{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems become harder to change because abstraction boundaries, once frozen into stable APIs, force every internal evolution to be expressed as a new API surface, causing unrelated changes to propagate across the system as each component must consume and adapt to the expanding interface. The premature freezing of these boundaries—before the true cost of future change is known—means that each locally correct modification calcifies the system further, since the API becomes a contract that couples components which would otherwise evolve independently."}
